{"name":"Spring Grass","hex":"#d5cb7f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or burst forth.","To appear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of springing: a leap, a jump.","The season of the year in temperate regions in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and plants spring from the ground and into bloom and dormant animals spring to life."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spring"},{"term":"grass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the family Poaceae, characterized by leaves that arise from nodes in the stem and leaf bases that wrap around the stem, especially those grown as ground cover rather than for grain.","Any of the various plants that are not in the family Poaceae that resemble grasses."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lay out on the grass; to knock down (an opponent etc.).","To act as a grass or informer, to betray; to report on (criminals etc) to the authorities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"spring":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/En-uk-spring.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/14/